        "China has over 60 laws and policies that address disability rights, including hearing loss. Enforcement is concentrated in institutional settings — workplaces, hospitals, and schools. In homes and social environments — where most of the communication gap between deaf and hard of hearing people and their hearing family members is actually felt — there is no system. Sign language interpretation is available in formal settings in major cities. At the family dinner table, between partners, in a corridor conversation with a hearing colleague, the deaf or hard of hearing person navigates alone.",
        "A study on sensory loss and social participation in China found that hearing loss is significantly associated with reduced social participation — fewer social contacts, lower participation in community activities, greater isolation than hearing peers. The rising prevalence projected through 2060 means this gap is growing without a corresponding growth in the infrastructure that would close it. Captio AI captions any hearing speaker's Chinese in real time — Mandarin or Cantonese, fast or slow — so a deaf or hard of hearing person follows every word at a family dinner, across a desk, or in any everyday setting.",
