        "Hungary is home to one of Europe's oldest deaf advocacy organisations: the Hungarian Association of the Deaf and Hard of Hearing (SINOSZ), founded in 1907, which has spent more than a century working to improve education, social integration, and communication access for deaf and hard-of-hearing Hungarians. Hungarian Sign Language (Magyar Jelnyelv) received formal legal recognition in 2009 under the Hungarian Sign Language Act — making Hungary one of the Central European countries to codify sign language rights. The Global Burden of Disease 2019 study found that hearing loss is the most prevalent cause of years lived with disability in Europe, affecting tens of millions across the continent, with prevalence increasing substantially with age.",
        "Despite SINOSZ's century of advocacy and the legal recognition of Hungarian Sign Language, access to real-time communication support in informal and community settings in Hungary remains very limited. Sign language interpretation is available for formal public contexts but is effectively absent from family social gatherings, birthday celebrations, community meetings, and neighbourhood events — the settings where Hungarian deaf and hard-of-hearing adults most frequently report feeling excluded from conversation. Hungarian's phonological complexity — 14 vowel phonemes distinguished by length, unique palatal consonants, and agglutinative suffix chains — makes lip-reading in group settings particularly demanding even for experienced lip-readers.",
        "Captio AI captions Hungarian conversation in real time on a phone screen — so a deaf family member at a karácsony dinner or a multigenerational gathering in Budapest, Debrecen, or a Hungarian diaspora event in Vienna or Toronto can follow every story, toast, and debate as it is spoken.",
